WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.081
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4490.

2
00:00:01.081 --> 00:00:02.163
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.163 --> 00:00:02.523
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Okay.

4
00:00:02.523 --> 00:00:07.930
So I’m Chelsea Arsenault with the [LSU Libraries] T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History.

5
00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.093
Today is . . . What is today’s date?

6
00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:10.814
The twenty-ninth?

7
00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:12.616
Today is July twenty-ninth, 2015.

8
00:00:12.616 --> 00:00:16.942
We are at Ms. Mildred Calvin Guidry’s home in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

9
00:00:16.942 --> 00:00:20.907
And we’re here to talk about her memories of Mossville, Louisiana,

10
00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:27.756
as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

11
00:00:27.756 --> 00:00:29.919
This is our first interview together.

12
00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:31.000
<v MILDRED CALVIN GUIDRY>Okay, okay.

13
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.747
<v ARSENEAULT>And I think it’s your first time being interviewed with the center.

14
00:00:33.747 --> 00:00:33.976
<v GUIDRY>Yeah.

15
00:00:33.976 --> 00:00:35.120
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, today is the thirtieth.

16
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:35.578
I’m sorry.

17
00:00:35.578 --> 00:00:36.723
Today is July thirtieth, 2015.

18
00:00:36.723 --> 00:00:38.554
<v GUIDRY>Yeah, because it’s the last day of July.

19
00:00:38.554 --> 00:00:39.699
<v ARSENEAULT>I think there’s one more.

20
00:00:39.699 --> 00:00:41.301
I think there might be one more.

21
00:00:41.301 --> 00:00:41.759
<v GUIDRY>One more?

22
00:00:41.759 --> 00:00:41.988
Tomorrow?

23
00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:43.819
<v ARSENEAULT>I think tomorrow’s the thirty . . . I think so.

24
00:00:43.819 --> 00:00:44.506
I’m almost positive.

25
00:00:44.506 --> 00:00:45.193
<v GUIDRY>Okay, okay, okay.

26
00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:47.253
Don’t be looking at my false eyelashes, because . . . [laughter]

27
00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:48.398
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, no. You look fine.

28
00:00:48.398 --> 00:00:49.084
You look fine.

29
00:00:49.084 --> 00:00:50.000
You look good.

30
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:57.480
<v GUIDRY>[laughs] You know, I don’t have them on right now.

31
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:12.439
I don’t use them . . . But one of the teachers said, “I’m telling you . . .” This is going to be on there.

32
00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:19.171
She said, “Oh, Mildred, what happened to your eyes?”

33
00:01:19.171 --> 00:01:25.902
I said, “I don’t have my false eye lashes.”

34
00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:31.886
“Oh, girl, don’t you wear those things anymore?”

35
00:01:31.886 --> 00:01:49.837
But this morning I got up late, and I had to put them on because I don’t have time to put the false on.

36
00:01:49.837 --> 00:01:52.081
<v ARSENEAULT>You look great.

37
00:01:52.081 --> 00:01:55.821
<v GUIDRY>Well, you see, I’m old.

38
00:01:55.821 --> 00:01:58.813
I’m eighty-one years old.

39
00:01:58.813 --> 00:02:03.301
So I’ll be eighty-two in September.

40
00:02:03.301 --> 00:02:07.041
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>You look good for eighty-one.

41
00:02:07.041 --> 00:02:09.285
<v GUIDRY>So that’s it.

42
00:02:09.285 --> 00:02:11.528
Okay, go on.

43
00:02:11.528 --> 00:02:12.276
Alright.

44
00:02:12.276 --> 00:02:13.024
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

45
00:02:13.024 --> 00:02:22.000
So go ahead and state your full name for the recording.

46
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:22.249
<v GUIDRY>Okay.

47
00:02:22.249 --> 00:02:23.744
My name is Mildred Calvin Guidry.

48
00:02:23.744 --> 00:02:26.982
At the time I was at Mossville, that was my first teaching experience.

49
00:02:26.982 --> 00:02:29.473
And I was a Calvin; my maiden name is Calvin.

50
00:02:29.473 --> 00:02:30.967
So that’s what I went to.

51
00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:33.209
I only went to Guidry after I got married.

52
00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:35.201
So therefore . . . But at my experience at Mossville,

53
00:02:35.201 --> 00:02:38.938
that was the most nicest in spirit—I want to use the word . . . take that word,

54
00:02:38.938 --> 00:02:41.179
I don’t want to say that—experience that I had.

55
00:02:41.179 --> 00:02:42.923
Because the environment, it was so nice.

56
00:02:42.923 --> 00:02:46.659
I enjoyed going to school every day, because the parents, they was real, real knowledgeable,

57
00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:53.136
they was concerned about their children and they weren’t concerned if the children come and say, “Ms. Calvin didn't do this, Ms. Calvin didn't do that.”

58
00:02:53.136 --> 00:02:55.377
They didn’t come up there get no Ms. Calvin.

59
00:02:55.377 --> 00:03:01.106
And it was . . . to me, the community to me, seemed to be that it was the parents were maybe from down east somewhere.

60
00:03:01.106 --> 00:03:03.597
Down like Lafayette [Louisiana] and so forth and like that.

61
00:03:03.597 --> 00:03:04.593
But they were concerned.

62
00:03:04.593 --> 00:03:10.322
I don't know if they all had gone to school, educated or not before, but they were very, very . . . And later in life,

63
00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:14.806
I could see they must . . . Mossville must have been built up and they had good teachers and all,

64
00:03:14.806 --> 00:03:20.286
because for one, the parents was very knowledgeable, and you know, they looked like they had gone to school and be there.

65
00:03:20.286 --> 00:03:24.769
And the community still . . . It looked like it was still kind of like a closed community, you know.

66
00:03:24.769 --> 00:03:26.015
It wasn’t integrated there before.

67
00:03:26.015 --> 00:03:27.509
They were all black back there.

68
00:03:27.509 --> 00:03:29.751
But when they closed Mossville, they was closing down.

69
00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:32.989
Because of the law—it was changing about integration—they sent me to Westwood [Elementary].

70
00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:37.722
That’s when the blacks had to go to the white school and the white came to the black school,

71
00:03:37.722 --> 00:03:40.711
and white went to the white, black went to the white school.

72
00:03:40.711 --> 00:03:42.205
And they sent me to Westwood.

73
00:03:42.205 --> 00:03:44.447
I’m telling you, when I went to Westwood that

74
00:03:44.447 --> 00:03:48.432
<c.p>was the most . . . I would hate to get up to go to work the next morning,

75
00:03:48.432 --> 00:03:50.674
because the way they treat, they didn’t respect you.

76
00:03:50.674 --> 00:03:58.645
And I have always loved . . . I didn’t care whether . . . I still now . . . I don’t be around and I see them in the store and all and I see a cute little baby,

77
00:03:58.645 --> 00:04:01.883
a cute little white baby, a little black, I say, “Oh . . .” I say,

78
00:04:01.883 --> 00:04:05.619
“Ma’am,” and the baby be looking sometime, the child look at me and just smile.

79
00:04:05.619 --> 00:04:07.363
I say, “Oh, he’s smiling at me.

80
00:04:07.363 --> 00:04:09.355
I must be . . . I must be not ugly.”

81
00:04:09.355 --> 00:04:10.352
And they just laugh.

82
00:04:10.352 --> 00:04:13.590
When I meet some of them, they’ll kind of look at me funny.

83
00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.832
I say, “I’m not trying to steal your baby.”

84
00:04:15.832 --> 00:04:19.070
I say, “But see how he’s not going to say anything to me.”

85
00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:24.300
I say, “You taught that child very, very good about life,” I said, “Because you taught him, don’t talk to strangers.”

86
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.044
I say, “That’s one of the things.”

87
00:04:26.044 --> 00:04:27.289
I said, “I can tell.”

88
00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:30.029
And I say, “Thank you very much for letting me talk.

89
00:04:30.029 --> 00:04:31.773
I said, “But I just love children.”

90
00:04:31.773 --> 00:04:33.267
And they say, “Oh, no, ma’am.

91
00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:34.015
Oh, no ma’am.”

92
00:04:34.015 --> 00:04:35.260
And the little children laugh.

93
00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.502
And some of them, they got a little frown.

94
00:04:37.502 --> 00:04:38.000
Okay.

95
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.667
But when I went to Westwood, I did the same thing to those children.

96
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:49.000
Because see, at Mossville, all . . . you all haven’t been there, but they had children as pretty, like you all,

97
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:55.000
blacks with the . . . it was just, if you all stayed in Mossville, most of them that got there.

98
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.667
But during the time that was there, they had some of the prettiest children.

99
00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:05.333
You know, the Fontenots, the Guidry, not mine, because my ex-husband, he was from Church Point [Louisiana].

100
00:05:05.333 --> 00:05:08.000
But his parents, they weren’t the same color.

101
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
His daddy was darker, but his mother looked like she was white.

102
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.667
You understand what I’m saying?

103
00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:17.000
So it was the idea . . . When I went to Westwood,

104
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.667
I was doing the same thing with these children, hugging them like I did my little black children over there.

105
00:05:23.667 --> 00:05:26.667
And they come . . . A little girl came to me.

106
00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:30.333
Every time I think about it, I just feel so sad.

107
00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:33.333
I wonder if that child is still living somewhere.

108
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:41.000
Her daddy . . . I think she came, and the next morning she said, “My daddy said, ‘don’t you put your nigger hands on me.’”

109
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.000
And here I’m running with tears in my eyes to Mr. Burns.

110
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.333
He was nice, though.

111
00:05:46.333 --> 00:05:48.000
But he was the principal.

112
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:48.333
He

113
00:05:48.333 --> 00:05:50.000
<c.p>say, “Ms. Guidry, I’m sorry.

114
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.333
Don’t feel like that.”

115
00:05:51.333 --> 00:05:58.333
I said, “Just the idea, this child going to tell me her daddy say, ‘don’t put her nigger hands on her.’

116
00:05:58.333 --> 00:06:00.000
And that was it.”

117
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.614
But then some of them was nice because . . . That’s the time I hadn’t made it with some.

118
00:06:05.614 --> 00:06:11.888
That was just one, I guess, man, that just didn’t have . . . some of them didn’t have . . . Like you all,

119
00:06:11.888 --> 00:06:17.171
you all probably good but you maybe have some way back there to talk about blacks.

120
00:06:17.171 --> 00:06:17.831
We’re human.

121
00:06:17.831 --> 00:06:19.813
We’re the color of our skin.

122
00:06:19.813 --> 00:06:26.087
We can learn, we can learn like you all, we can think, but we just wasn’t exposed to it.

123
00:06:26.087 --> 00:06:28.728
But that’s what I say about Mossville children.

124
00:06:28.728 --> 00:06:35.002
Those people looked like they were experienced, whoever, that coming up, the way they were seeing about their children.

125
00:06:35.002 --> 00:06:37.314
They want the best for their children.

126
00:06:37.314 --> 00:06:39.295
They was concerned about their children.

127
00:06:39.295 --> 00:06:44.248
And if you all observe them now, how close all of them are grown up.

128
00:06:44.248 --> 00:06:46.890
And all of them, mostly, have done well.

129
00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:51.183
You know, they left the Mossville, they stayed there till Mossville High School.

130
00:06:51.183 --> 00:06:56.466
And when they went to Mossville High School, they went to Westlake High School, I think.

131
00:06:56.466 --> 00:06:57.787
That’s where they went.

132
00:06:57.787 --> 00:06:58.778
I don't know.

133
00:06:58.778 --> 00:07:03.731
See, I was in first, I was in elementary, so I don't know about there.

134
00:07:03.731 --> 00:07:07.693
But you could tell then about . . . That’s what I want to say.

135
00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:13.637
The community out there, if you all stayed out there you could see the close encounter like them,

136
00:07:13.637 --> 00:07:17.930
so it would be in a rural area where they came up at.

137
00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:22.883
And like most of them, I’m saying it over, most of them really done well.

138
00:07:22.883 --> 00:07:26.845
Because I think the ones that I taught . . . I taught first grade.

139
00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:28.496
I think she was there.

140
00:07:28.496 --> 00:07:30.148
But only one was there.

141
00:07:30.148 --> 00:07:32.129
But he wasn’t the best one.

142
00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:36.752
I have children that I taught in the first grade, some of them nurses.

143
00:07:36.752 --> 00:07:40.054
One, I think, one is a doctor, but she’s white.

144
00:07:40.054 --> 00:07:43.026
She came from Westwood, when I taught at Westwood.

145
00:07:43.026 --> 00:07:45.007
She was in the first grade.

146
00:07:45.007 --> 00:07:47.649
She was black, but she . . . She’s a doctor.

147
00:07:47.649 --> 00:07:50.951
She went to school, I think, somewhere in Shreveport, Louisiana.

148
00:07:50.951 --> 00:07:54.913
There’s a college up there where they teach medical somewhere up there.

149
00:07:54.913 --> 00:07:56.234
That’s where she went.

150
00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:58.546
And all of them have done well.

151
00:07:58.546 --> 00:08:00.197
Some of them are teaching.

152
00:08:00.197 --> 00:08:01.187
Because one of

153
00:08:01.187 --> 00:08:04.820
<c.p>them went to McNeese [State University] and majored in elementary education.

154
00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:10.763
And she wanted to come to observe me to observe, because they have to go in to observe.

155
00:08:10.763 --> 00:08:18.358
You know, you say you’re a teacher you know you have to go, so she asked could she come in and observe me.

156
00:08:18.358 --> 00:08:21.000
And they all just love Ms. Calvin.

157
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.047
But I wasn’t Ms. Calvin then.

158
00:08:23.047 --> 00:08:24.411
I was Ms. Guidry.

159
00:08:24.411 --> 00:08:28.505
I didn’t get to be Ms. Guidry until I married my ex-husband.

160
00:08:28.505 --> 00:08:32.598
I go with Guidry because my daughter, when she was a baby,

161
00:08:32.598 --> 00:08:38.056
I didn’t want my daughter going to school being a Guidry, and I was a Calvin.

162
00:08:38.056 --> 00:08:40.103
So I just kept his name.

163
00:08:40.103 --> 00:08:44.196
I kept it and I’m going to die with it, you know.

164
00:08:44.196 --> 00:08:46.584
I’m going to die with his name.

165
00:08:46.584 --> 00:08:48.631
So that’s the way I am.

166
00:08:48.631 --> 00:08:55.794
So a lot of them talking, they’d use, most of them, Ms. Calvin, because a lot of them really don’t know.

167
00:08:55.794 --> 00:09:02.958
Now they say, “Well, I asked somebody did they know Ms. Guidry, they say no, they don’t remember no Ms. Guidry.”

168
00:09:02.958 --> 00:09:05.005
I say, well, they don’t remember.

169
00:09:05.005 --> 00:09:08.757
I say, “Well, you ask them do they remember Ms. Calvin.”

170
00:09:08.757 --> 00:09:11.827
I say, “Next time . . .” So we take exercise together.

171
00:09:11.827 --> 00:09:14.556
They said, “Well, we remember that Ms. Calvin.

172
00:09:14.556 --> 00:09:17.626
She was pretty and she wore the prettiest clothes.”

173
00:09:17.626 --> 00:09:19.673
I was young then, I did.

174
00:09:19.673 --> 00:09:21.379
I wasn’t fat and all.

175
00:09:21.379 --> 00:09:22.402
I was trim.

176
00:09:22.402 --> 00:09:25.131
I was nice, like you all and everything.

177
00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:27.519
They say, “That’s the one I know.”

178
00:09:27.519 --> 00:09:29.565
I said, “Well you tell him.

179
00:09:29.565 --> 00:09:34.000
Tell your cousin or whoever it was, does he remember Ms. Calvin?”

180
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.230
I say that all the high school boys used to be down to my room.

181
00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:43.065
Because one time . . . no, I better not put that on there.

182
00:09:43.065 --> 00:09:47.946
But the principal had to call me in her office and say, “Ms. Guidry,

183
00:09:47.946 --> 00:09:56.662
your mother will not like me to write to her that you was fired because you was messing around with a high school . . . ” I said,

184
00:09:56.662 --> 00:09:57.708
“What you mean?”

185
00:09:57.708 --> 00:10:00.149
She said, “Well, that’s what they’re saying.

186
00:10:00.149 --> 00:10:05.378
You’re going with George Keller,” which was a very . . . he was a high school student.

187
00:10:05.378 --> 00:10:07.819
He was very famous for playing basketball.

188
00:10:07.819 --> 00:10:15.141
But he was living . . . he went to school because Mossville at the time had a good team in basketball and football.

189
00:10:15.141 --> 00:10:16.884
But he was living over

190
00:10:16.884 --> 00:10:17.232
<c.p>here.

191
00:10:17.232 --> 00:10:20.719
But therefore, I was living, I had moved over here.

192
00:10:20.719 --> 00:10:24.554
So he asked me could he get a ride with me.

193
00:10:24.554 --> 00:10:26.646
And I gave him a ride.

194
00:10:26.646 --> 00:10:30.830
So somebody saw this, they thought something was going on between us.

195
00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:34.665
So I said, “Ms. Washington, please . . .” She was a lady then.

196
00:10:34.665 --> 00:10:39.197
I said, “Please don’t do that to me,” I said, “Because it’s not.”

197
00:10:39.197 --> 00:10:47.216
I said, “A young man say his mother couldn’t pick him up and he ask me could he ride with me going home.”

198
00:10:47.216 --> 00:10:48.611
I said, “That’s it.”

199
00:10:48.611 --> 00:10:51.400
She said, “Because your mother wouldn’t like that.

200
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:52.795
She wouldn’t like that.”

201
00:10:52.795 --> 00:10:55.235
I said, “No, she wouldn’t like that.”

202
00:10:55.235 --> 00:11:02.208
So that’s what, that part. [TS] But everything else, the parents, the community, their closed community and the same old,

203
00:11:02.208 --> 00:11:07.089
like I stated before . . . Don’t put all that what I’m saying, that joking stuff.

204
00:11:07.089 --> 00:11:15.108
What I said before that they were closed to be, that small community, how they got around, and how they believed in things.

205
00:11:15.108 --> 00:11:21.035
Like how they stressing to you all how they want Mossville to be remembered by going there.

206
00:11:21.035 --> 00:11:26.614
I think it wasn’t built up like such and such, and how they built it up.

207
00:11:26.614 --> 00:11:31.146
And they still trying to hold that up the way it was before.

208
00:11:31.146 --> 00:11:32.192
So that’s it.

209
00:11:32.192 --> 00:11:35.678
And that was my best years at Mossville High School.

210
00:11:35.678 --> 00:11:37.770
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did you teach there?

211
00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:41.605
<v GUIDRY>I taught there for like . . . till it crossed over to blacks.

212
00:11:41.605 --> 00:11:43.000
I don't remember.

213
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.038
<v ARSENEAULT>Let me see.

214
00:11:44.038 --> 00:11:46.808
<v GUIDRY>All I had to [tell her?] . . . You see . . .

215
00:11:46.808 --> 00:11:49.577
<v ARSENEAULT>So you taught at . . . Is this in order?

216
00:11:49.577 --> 00:11:50.269
<v GUIDRY>That’s right.

217
00:11:50.269 --> 00:11:50.962
Mossville first.

218
00:11:50.962 --> 00:11:52.692
<v ARSENEAULT>So you taught Mossville first.

219
00:11:52.692 --> 00:11:53.385
<v GUIDRY>And Westwood.

220
00:11:53.385 --> 00:11:55.115
You got Westwood on there?

221
00:11:55.115 --> 00:11:55.462
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

222
00:11:55.462 --> 00:11:58.923
So you taught . . . What year did you start at Mossville?

223
00:11:58.923 --> 00:12:02.385
<v GUIDRY>Mossville, when I was first out of college, was nineteen.

224
00:12:02.385 --> 00:12:03.423
I don't remember.

225
00:12:03.423 --> 00:12:05.846
Nineteen . . . I’ve been a long time ago.

226
00:12:05.846 --> 00:12:14.500
I remember . . . but it was after I taught there until . . . if you go back and talk to somebody else and see how long Mossville . . . See,

227
00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:15.885
Mossville, they closed Mossville.

228
00:12:15.885 --> 00:12:21.077
And when the turnover was, that’s when they start transferring us to these different schools.

229
00:12:21.077 --> 00:12:25.577
That’s why my supervisor sent me to Westwood and I stayed there until . . .

230
00:12:25.577 --> 00:12:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So Mossville was an all-black school.

231
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:28.980
<v GUIDRY>Yeah, all-black school.

232
00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.918
<v ARSENEAULT>And then you taught there for a few years.

233
00:12:31.918 --> 00:12:35.184
<v GUIDRY>I taught there until it was time for the crossover.

234
00:12:35.184 --> 00:12:35.510
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

235
00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:38.122
And then you had to go to Westwood.

236
00:12:38.122 --> 00:12:38.776
<v GUIDRY>That’s right.

237
00:12:38.776 --> 00:12:41.714
<v ARSENEAULT>Because they needed . . . why did you have to go . . .

238
00:12:41.714 --> 00:12:42.041
<v GUIDRY>Wait.

239
00:12:42.041 --> 00:12:43.347
Let me explain that.

240
00:12:43.347 --> 00:12:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

241
00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.791
<v GUIDRY>I was hired . . . Mossville was the main school.

242
00:12:46.791 --> 00:12:54.814
But when they turned that over, your supervisor came and saw where would you be best, I guess, when it was transferring me.

243
00:12:54.814 --> 00:12:57.256
So they needed a teacher at Westwood.

244
00:12:57.256 --> 00:12:59.698
That’s why they sent me at Westwood.

245
00:12:59.698 --> 00:13:05.279
And when I left Westwood, that’s when I got tired of being . . . When I heard that,

246
00:13:05.279 --> 00:13:08.419
I didn’t feel like going to work at all.

247
00:13:08.419 --> 00:13:12.953
I didn't feel it because feeling was somebody . . . Just like you all now.

248
00:13:12.953 --> 00:13:18.884
And if you don’t want me there and you had that, why would I be happy there?

249
00:13:18.884 --> 00:13:24.116
So I kept on until I found me a first grade at Cherry Street [School].

250
00:13:24.116 --> 00:13:29.000
If you see Cherry Street on there, that’s where I went to Cherry Street.

251
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.791
And that’s where I retired from Cherry Street.

252
00:13:31.791 --> 00:13:33.186
I retired from there.

253
00:13:33.186 --> 00:13:41.907
Because I would have stayed at Westwood, but after that child came there and said that, well I didn’t have no more problem, you know.

254
00:13:41.907 --> 00:13:44.000
Because I had gotten there . . .

255
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.292
<v ARSENEAULT>What else happened there?

256
00:13:45.292 --> 00:13:47.553
<v GUIDRY>Let me tell you, now, about Westwood.

257
00:13:47.553 --> 00:13:49.814
There was just one person prejudiced there.

258
00:13:49.814 --> 00:13:56.274
The parents start . . . they would come and have . . . room mother would come and do everything, just like fix everything up.

259
00:13:56.274 --> 00:13:57.243
You’re a teacher.

260
00:13:57.243 --> 00:13:59.504
You know how the room mother do.

261
00:13:59.504 --> 00:14:04.027
Okay, plus the fact that’s the time I was married and had my daughter.

262
00:14:04.027 --> 00:14:06.934
My daughter went to a private school, Saint Margaret.

263
00:14:06.934 --> 00:14:08.549
Saint Margaret was a private.

264
00:14:08.549 --> 00:14:10.487
They had to wear little uniforms.

265
00:14:10.487 --> 00:14:15.009
And at the time, it was ideal that I couldn’t pay for those uniforms.

266
00:14:15.009 --> 00:14:22.115
I had a lady, one of my ladies in there . . . I wished I know where Lisa’s mother is now, where they are.

267
00:14:22.115 --> 00:14:27.283
She liked me so well, I guess she did, she showed it, and she could sew.

268
00:14:27.283 --> 00:14:31.159
And so therefore I asked her to make my daughter’s uniform skirts,

269
00:14:31.159 --> 00:14:36.327
because it was hard for me, paying that, because I wanted me daughter to go there.

270
00:14:36.327 --> 00:14:41.819
But they couldn’t pay for the . . . They helped me not to pay the lunch for my child,

271
00:14:41.819 --> 00:14:45.049
because we are Catholic and it was a Catholic school.

272
00:14:45.049 --> 00:14:49.248
So therefore, that parent did all of my little uniform for my child.

273
00:14:49.248 --> 00:14:55.385
That’s the way it . . . And then I would have them to come sometime and just do parties and thing.

274
00:14:55.385 --> 00:14:57.000
That was just one.

275
00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:02.339
I cannot say all the Westwood parents in that area was prejudiced, because I can’t.

276
00:15:02.339 --> 00:15:06.966
Because there was some . . . That was just that one child father said that.

277
00:15:06.966 --> 00:15:09.102
And he was a poor person.

278
00:15:09.102 --> 00:15:12.305
He didn’t [worship?] and he just didn’t understand it.

279
00:15:12.305 --> 00:15:16.220
I don’t believe because all whites is just not like that.

280
00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:17.644
It’s just like that.

281
00:15:17.644 --> 00:15:24.407
Because of where I am today, and when I came, went to Mossville, I had been, gone to college.

282
00:15:24.407 --> 00:15:27.254
But who helped me to go to college?

283
00:15:27.254 --> 00:15:32.237
Who helped me when I had stopped school one time in the sixth grade?

284
00:15:32.237 --> 00:15:36.508
Who helped me to talk to me to go back to school?

285
00:15:36.508 --> 00:15:38.288
It was a white lady.

286
00:15:38.288 --> 00:15:41.492
And how she got her job way back there?

287
00:15:41.492 --> 00:15:47.186
If you wanted a job, any kind of job, you went to the social security office.

288
00:15:47.186 --> 00:15:49.322
And that lady needed a maid.

289
00:15:49.322 --> 00:15:55.017
And what happened while I came down here, she saw me and I went and applied.

290
00:15:55.017 --> 00:15:56.085
She hired me.

291
00:15:56.085 --> 00:15:56.441
But

292
00:15:56.441 --> 00:16:00.712
<c.p>she was also had moved back, she taught, her teacher was young,

293
00:16:00.712 --> 00:16:07.831
her teaching experience was right here in Rapides Parish at Darby Elementary. [TS] But when . . . You all probably wasn’t born,

294
00:16:07.831 --> 00:16:14.237
but one time it had a big old, everybody think they just was messed up in Cameron [Parish].

295
00:16:14.237 --> 00:16:14.949
You understand?

296
00:16:14.949 --> 00:16:18.864
So she and her husband, they moved back to Alexandria [Louisiana].

297
00:16:18.864 --> 00:16:22.424
And that’s the way I got in touch with them.

298
00:16:22.424 --> 00:16:24.915
I mean, that’s the way she got.

299
00:16:24.915 --> 00:16:30.966
And she saw a little something good in me, I guess, because I was young, you know.

300
00:16:30.966 --> 00:16:32.390
But something in me.

301
00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.525
And so she just liked me.

302
00:16:34.525 --> 00:16:40.576
So she was . . . She had moved back to Alexandria because they didn't want to live here anymore,

303
00:16:40.576 --> 00:16:43.068
because they experienced that kind of weather.

304
00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:45.203
They couldn’t go through that anymore.

305
00:16:45.203 --> 00:16:48.407
So therefore, she and her husband moved back there.

306
00:16:48.407 --> 00:16:52.322
So I went back there and I was working for them.

307
00:16:52.322 --> 00:16:54.458
So they were going to school.

308
00:16:54.458 --> 00:17:00.864
And I was nurse baby, keeping their babies, you know, while they went to work on their master’s.

309
00:17:00.864 --> 00:17:02.644
So then she saw that.

310
00:17:02.644 --> 00:17:05.136
And she knew I had stopped school.

311
00:17:05.136 --> 00:17:11.186
She . . . what you call . . . she made me, she said, “You’re going to go back to school, Mildred.

312
00:17:11.186 --> 00:17:12.966
You’re going back to school.”

313
00:17:12.966 --> 00:17:15.102
And I went back to school.

314
00:17:15.102 --> 00:17:15.814
Finished school.

315
00:17:15.814 --> 00:17:19.373
Went on, went and finished, finished high school and everything.

316
00:17:19.373 --> 00:17:24.000
And then after that it was time for me to . . . [phone rings]

317
00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:25.390
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you need to . . .

318
00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:27.823
<v GUIDRY>That’s okay, baby, just let it ring.

319
00:17:27.823 --> 00:17:32.688
So anyway, it was time for me to . . . [phone message kicks in] Who’s calling?

320
00:17:32.688 --> 00:17:35.121
<v DIGILORMO>You can get it if you like.

321
00:17:35.121 --> 00:17:36.858
You want to get it?

322
00:17:36.858 --> 00:17:39.291
<v GUIDRY>See who that is on there, baby.

323
00:17:39.291 --> 00:17:40.681
<v DIGILORMO>It says Mildred Guidry.

324
00:17:40.681 --> 00:17:41.028
<v GUIDRY>Really?

325
00:17:41.028 --> 00:17:42.418
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s you. [phone ringing]

326
00:17:42.418 --> 00:17:43.809
<v DIGILORMO>Let me pause this.

327
00:17:43.809 --> 00:17:48.674
<v GUIDRY>Hello? [Break in tape] Go back and see, she told me about down here.

328
00:17:48.674 --> 00:17:52.844
Because see, she taught at Darby, and she told me about this.

329
00:17:52.844 --> 00:17:57.362
So she helped me when I went on back and finished high school.

330
00:17:57.362 --> 00:17:59.447
Because they laughed at me again.

331
00:17:59.447 --> 00:18:06.050
Not . . . No, the children I had gone to start school with, they had gone on, gone on to school.

332
00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:08.830
But I came up so very, very poor.

333
00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:14.738
My mother had three of us and she didn’t have any . . . She didn't have an education, either.

334
00:18:14.738 --> 00:18:22.035
And she tried her best to raise us and, you know, at that time, we didn’t have no kind of help.

335
00:18:22.035 --> 00:18:27.248
But anyway, whites always helped her because she worked for the nuns; we was Catholic.

336
00:18:27.248 --> 00:18:28.986
So she worked with them.

337
00:18:28.986 --> 00:18:32.809
Food and stuff, when they had all . . . they was over schools.

338
00:18:32.809 --> 00:18:39.411
And they gave my mother a lot, a lot of food and stuff to help her and all that.

339
00:18:39.411 --> 00:18:44.624
And then, okay, I went on and I remember Mrs. Stuart gave me this dress.

340
00:18:44.624 --> 00:18:48.794
I was in . . . went to school and I was in this play.

341
00:18:48.794 --> 00:18:53.312
And all these children was . . . they were all . . . They were little bitty children.

342
00:18:53.312 --> 00:18:57.482
But I was grown for my age because I had stopped school.

343
00:18:57.482 --> 00:19:02.348
And then I, you know . . . Going back, that was like in the third grade.

344
00:19:02.348 --> 00:19:05.475
So this teacher made me be a queen sitting

345
00:19:05.475 --> 00:19:07.560
<c.p>there looking at us sitting there.

346
00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:11.383
And the children around me looking, you know, doing everything else.

347
00:19:11.383 --> 00:19:13.468
But I was like the queen.

348
00:19:13.468 --> 00:19:18.333
Because the dress Ms. Stuart had got me a big old beautiful dress, red.

349
00:19:18.333 --> 00:19:21.461
And I was . . . Because I always be liking clothing.

350
00:19:21.461 --> 00:19:23.894
And I was all pretty and everything.

351
00:19:23.894 --> 00:19:31.539
And then my students, my classmates, the people that I knew from my community, of course their parents was able to do [?],

352
00:19:31.539 --> 00:19:37.099
they was laughing at me, “Oh, look at Mildred up there with them little bitty children.”

353
00:19:37.099 --> 00:19:39.532
They just laughed and laughed and laughed.

354
00:19:39.532 --> 00:19:41.965
But it didn't make me feel bad.

355
00:19:41.965 --> 00:19:45.440
I just sit there right like I’m looking at you.

356
00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:49.262
I sit there, but I was kind of ashamed, though [laughs].

357
00:19:49.262 --> 00:19:51.000
But I went on.

358
00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.379
Ms. Stuart helped me.

359
00:19:52.379 --> 00:19:55.482
Her name was Mrs. Stuart and she helped me.

360
00:19:55.482 --> 00:19:59.619
And then I said that I’m not able to go to college.

361
00:19:59.619 --> 00:20:02.377
That’s when I went on, finished high school.

362
00:20:02.377 --> 00:20:04.790
I’m not able to go to college.

363
00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:05.824
She helped me.

364
00:20:05.824 --> 00:20:06.858
You know what?

365
00:20:06.858 --> 00:20:07.893
She helped me.

366
00:20:07.893 --> 00:20:11.685
My mother wasn’t able to send me like clothes and things.

367
00:20:11.685 --> 00:20:18.925
She sent me a little money to buy the private stuff, like makeup and . . . not makeup, you know, sanitary napkins, that.

368
00:20:18.925 --> 00:20:21.683
But she wasn’t able to just send anything.

369
00:20:21.683 --> 00:20:23.751
You know what she would do?

370
00:20:23.751 --> 00:20:28.233
If your parents were rich and it was her friend, she would call.

371
00:20:28.233 --> 00:20:34.438
When I would come home, she would come over and say, “Well, what you need this time, Mildred?”

372
00:20:34.438 --> 00:20:37.886
I say, “I need . . .” Don’t you put this on it.

373
00:20:37.886 --> 00:20:39.954
I say, “I need some stuff.

374
00:20:39.954 --> 00:20:41.678
Sanitary napkins and all that.”

375
00:20:41.678 --> 00:20:44.436
And then if I needed some panties, underwear,

376
00:20:44.436 --> 00:20:50.986
she’d ask her friend did they have some pair of underwear that they use that they didn’t want anymore.

377
00:20:50.986 --> 00:20:52.365
And things that . . . clothes.

378
00:20:52.365 --> 00:20:55.123
That’s the way I made it through college.

379
00:20:55.123 --> 00:20:55.813
You understand?

380
00:20:55.813 --> 00:21:00.295
But I went to Disciples of Christ, and that was a school, too.

381
00:21:00.295 --> 00:21:03.053
I worked my way through school, too, college.

382
00:21:03.053 --> 00:21:05.121
You heard of Jarvis Christian College?

383
00:21:05.121 --> 00:21:07.534
Well, that’s a Disciples of Christ school.

384
00:21:07.534 --> 00:21:12.705
And it was a very, very rich school because it had all [?] , all over it.

385
00:21:12.705 --> 00:21:15.119
It’s ninety-six miles east of Dallas [Texas].

386
00:21:15.119 --> 00:21:18.566
But Jarvis Christian College now has grown a lot now.

387
00:21:18.566 --> 00:21:19.256
It’s ninety-six

388
00:21:19.256 --> 00:21:20.635
<c.p>miles east of Dallas.

389
00:21:20.635 --> 00:21:23.048
And that’s where, when I went there,

390
00:21:23.048 --> 00:21:30.288
I also had a job because I didn't have anybody to send me money like, you know, to send me nothing.

391
00:21:30.288 --> 00:21:34.769
And so therefore they gave me a job there and I worked there.

392
00:21:34.769 --> 00:21:40.285
And then for, to come down here, Mrs. Stuart helped me with . . . we say application there,

393
00:21:40.285 --> 00:21:43.043
but you all say different things, not résumé.

394
00:21:43.043 --> 00:21:52.007
She helped me to do this résumé to come down here and start teaching down here because she knew about it because she taught down here.

395
00:21:52.007 --> 00:21:54.420
And that’s the reason I’m down here.

396
00:21:54.420 --> 00:22:00.281
And when I sent that there, that’s when I got it back that I had a job.

397
00:22:00.281 --> 00:22:02.694
And you see, I had a job.

398
00:22:02.694 --> 00:22:04.073
They signed the contract.

399
00:22:04.073 --> 00:22:12.692
But at the time, since I had finished gotten my degree in Texas, the Calcasieu Parish School Board would put on my contract every year,

400
00:22:12.692 --> 00:22:22.000
they would put on there, “If you do not go to school and work on your certificate to teach in Louisiana, you wouldn’t have a job.”

401
00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:26.055
So I had to go to . . . I went to Grambling State University,

402
00:22:26.055 --> 00:22:31.462
and I would go every summer until I got certified to be [care of?] my job.

403
00:22:31.462 --> 00:22:33.151
And that’s what happened there.

404
00:22:33.151 --> 00:22:37.206
But then, before I went, before, I’m kind of . . . what’s it called?

405
00:22:37.206 --> 00:22:38.220
I’m getting old.

406
00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.937
But before I came down here, though, they had hired me.

407
00:22:41.937 --> 00:22:44.640
She had helped me to get a job.

408
00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:51.060
Because see Ms. Stuart was like that, she was a person, her parents, they was kind of . . . They’re white.

409
00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:54.102
And they had a lot of real estate stuff.

410
00:22:54.102 --> 00:22:57.481
So therefore it wasn’t difficult for her to get around.

411
00:22:57.481 --> 00:23:01.198
But she had gotten around good in Alexandria, raised herself up.

412
00:23:01.198 --> 00:23:05.253
But she must have didn’t like Alexandria, then, to come down here.

413
00:23:05.253 --> 00:23:07.956
But anyway, what else I want to say?

414
00:23:07.956 --> 00:23:08.632
I forgot.

415
00:23:08.632 --> 00:23:08.970
Whatever.

416
00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:14.038
But anyway, she helped me to come down here and get my job and everything.

417
00:23:14.038 --> 00:23:15.052
And what else?

418
00:23:15.052 --> 00:23:18.431
And then when my baby was born, that was before,

419
00:23:18.431 --> 00:23:24.176
she and her husband came down here and they saw that the baby was born and everything.

420
00:23:24.176 --> 00:23:32.286
But then she told me, she said, “Mildred, I told John when we was back to Alexandria, that marriage not going to last long.

421
00:23:32.286 --> 00:23:35.327
Because you see, he think he smarter than Mildred.”

422
00:23:35.327 --> 00:23:36.679
And I said, “[Yes].”

423
00:23:36.679 --> 00:23:37.354
I said, [ . . . ?].

424
00:23:37.354 --> 00:23:38.706
She said, “It’s not

425
00:23:38.706 --> 00:23:40.396
<c.p>going to last very long.”

426
00:23:40.396 --> 00:23:42.423
And it didn’t last very long.

427
00:23:42.423 --> 00:23:47.492
It was the idea, I thought then maybe he thought that I had a job.

428
00:23:47.492 --> 00:23:50.871
And maybe he married me because I had a job.

429
00:23:50.871 --> 00:23:57.629
Because he was still in . . . I met him, he had been, he had a degree, he went to Southern [University].

430
00:23:57.629 --> 00:24:03.712
But I went there because I didn’t have the money to go and I didn’t get a scholarship.

431
00:24:03.712 --> 00:24:07.766
I wasn’t smart enough to get a scholarship or nothing to help.

432
00:24:07.766 --> 00:24:13.849
You all don’t know, because you all didn’t have to go through that about no help from there.

433
00:24:13.849 --> 00:24:15.201
You’d go to school.

434
00:24:15.201 --> 00:24:19.255
Your parents was able to send you to school, pay that tuition.

435
00:24:19.255 --> 00:24:22.635
Like now . . . Well no, my ex-husband, he paid child support.

436
00:24:22.635 --> 00:24:25.000
But then, that’s all he paid.

437
00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:29.352
But I had to pay for when Monica went to this Catholic school.

438
00:24:29.352 --> 00:24:30.692
I paid for that.

439
00:24:30.692 --> 00:24:34.374
Because I didn't want my child to come up like me.

440
00:24:34.374 --> 00:24:40.066
I did not want her . . . I wanted her to be the . . . the best I could give her.

441
00:24:40.066 --> 00:24:41.405
I could do that.

442
00:24:41.405 --> 00:24:45.423
That’s the way . . . And it’s [struggle?] for me to pay this tuition.

443
00:24:45.423 --> 00:24:47.097
You understand what I’m saying?

444
00:24:47.097 --> 00:24:48.101
And that’s why.

445
00:24:48.101 --> 00:24:50.110
But she . . . It wasn’t thing now.

446
00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:55.802
Because time she finished at McNeese, due to the job that she has, she finished in accounting.

447
00:24:55.802 --> 00:25:01.493
She looked . . . Her daddy saw it in the paper that they needed . . . and he went right on.

448
00:25:01.493 --> 00:25:04.507
Because in accounting at that time when she finished,

449
00:25:04.507 --> 00:25:12.542
a black person had to go to a big city to make a living off there, because they didn’t want to hire nobody black.

450
00:25:12.542 --> 00:25:15.220
But she was able to get a job.

451
00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:21.916
And she been there, and then lucky, the superintendent, got a new superintendent of Calcasieu Parish School Board this year.

452
00:25:21.916 --> 00:25:25.599
And when she first went there, it was two in accounting.

453
00:25:25.599 --> 00:25:27.273
A black and a white.

454
00:25:27.273 --> 00:25:28.947
The white was over her.

455
00:25:28.947 --> 00:25:32.630
And she was a second, the white girl was the first.

456
00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:37.652
Okay, when they elected Mister, whatever his name is, I’m going to say Mr. Superintendent.

457
00:25:37.652 --> 00:25:43.678
Therefore, they took his job, gave it to that high girl that had been there so many years.

458
00:25:43.678 --> 00:25:44.348
You understand?

459
00:25:44.348 --> 00:25:49.035
So therefore, her job, when Monica was like working under her a little bit,

460
00:25:49.035 --> 00:25:53.722
they gave her, before they hired somebody, they gave her job to my daughter.

461
00:25:53.722 --> 00:25:55.731
<c.p>And that’s what she does now.

462
00:25:55.731 --> 00:25:57.070
And she been there.

463
00:25:57.070 --> 00:26:00.419
And see, I’m so happy, because she been there now.

464
00:26:00.419 --> 00:26:05.441
If her daddy does . . . her husband, if he does her like her daddy did me,

465
00:26:05.441 --> 00:26:10.797
she’ll be able to take care of herself and be able to take care of hers.

466
00:26:10.797 --> 00:26:12.471
And she has two boys.

467
00:26:12.471 --> 00:26:19.502
And if you got to take care of those two boys, and then she’ll be able to take care of herself.

468
00:26:19.502 --> 00:26:23.185
Because you see . . . Don’t you put all this what I’m telling.

469
00:26:23.185 --> 00:26:26.868
See, when my ex-husband left me, we had built this house.

470
00:26:26.868 --> 00:26:28.542
We had built this house.

471
00:26:28.542 --> 00:26:33.564
Because all of these people on this street most is from here, about three houses,

472
00:26:33.564 --> 00:26:38.921
two or three houses on the other street, we were all teachers at Mossville High School.

473
00:26:38.921 --> 00:26:40.260
And this was land.

474
00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:45.952
When you see that general there, this used to be like somebody’s houses you see, maybe smaller.

475
00:26:45.952 --> 00:26:50.639
That used to be where all these people in the army used to live.

476
00:26:50.639 --> 00:26:53.987
That’s why they say General Marston, General Patton, all that.

477
00:26:53.987 --> 00:26:55.661
You understand what I’m saying?

478
00:26:55.661 --> 00:26:56.330
You understand?

479
00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

480
00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.293
<v GUIDRY>But this, my neighbor, she’s dead now.

481
00:26:59.293 --> 00:27:04.535
She called me and she said . . . We . . . was my coworker, she was the librarian at Mossville.

482
00:27:04.535 --> 00:27:12.726
She sent me over to say they got some vacant land by my house and said, “I would like for you to be my neighbor.”

483
00:27:12.726 --> 00:27:16.330
And I said okay and I went to see about it.

484
00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:17.968
And I built this house.

485
00:27:17.968 --> 00:27:21.900
My ex-husband, when he didn’t . . . don’t you put all this in there.

486
00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:26.487
He was going to . . . He told me . . . We just . . . You know the closing costs,

487
00:27:26.487 --> 00:27:32.056
you all not married still yet, to buy your homes you have to pay that closing cost.

488
00:27:32.056 --> 00:27:34.022
Your parents . . . You know, closing cost.

489
00:27:34.022 --> 00:27:37.626
So we had to pay like eight thousand dollars closing cost.

490
00:27:37.626 --> 00:27:39.919
And she said, “Well, I’ll tell you.

491
00:27:39.919 --> 00:27:44.834
You give me four thousand dollars, I’ll give you four thousand dollars, and that’s it.”

492
00:27:44.834 --> 00:27:46.800
I didn’t know where to turn.

493
00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:49.093
<c.p>But see, God has always blessed me.

494
00:27:49.093 --> 00:27:50.731
I turned to my mother.

495
00:27:50.731 --> 00:27:53.352
And my mother always . . . we were dirty poor.

496
00:27:53.352 --> 00:28:01.543
But she told us if we get a little job, if you make ten cents, you save five and you, you know, spend the five.

497
00:28:01.543 --> 00:28:03.181
That’s the way she was.

498
00:28:03.181 --> 00:28:07.112
So there when I called, I said, “I need four thousand dollars.”

499
00:28:07.112 --> 00:28:08.423
She said, “Four thousand?

500
00:28:08.423 --> 00:28:12.682
Who do you think I . . .” I said, “Well, I need four thousand dollars,

501
00:28:12.682 --> 00:28:16.614
or else Monica and I are going to be on the street.”

502
00:28:16.614 --> 00:28:18.252
And she said, “All right.

503
00:28:18.252 --> 00:28:20.873
You’re going to pay me my money back?”

504
00:28:20.873 --> 00:28:23.822
And I said, “Now, don’t do me like that.

505
00:28:23.822 --> 00:28:26.443
You know I’ve never borrowed anything from you.”

506
00:28:26.443 --> 00:28:28.408
I said, “But you know what?

507
00:28:28.408 --> 00:28:31.357
You work hard for that little money you have.

508
00:28:31.357 --> 00:28:33.978
And I’m going to pay your money back.”

509
00:28:33.978 --> 00:28:37.582
I borrowed the money from my mother, I paid him back.

510
00:28:37.582 --> 00:28:39.875
I got him out of this house.

511
00:28:39.875 --> 00:28:44.790
So therefore, got through paying for this house, I was able to pay this house.

512
00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:47.083
This is my house, and everything else.

513
00:28:47.083 --> 00:28:52.980
He took that he was going to make . . . so he . . . See, his wife now, she had four children.

514
00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:58.878
And he took her, was going to make her children comfortable in this house and not his blood.

515
00:28:58.878 --> 00:29:01.499
But he didn’t have the experience of that.

516
00:29:01.499 --> 00:29:03.465
So therefore, I raised my child.

517
00:29:03.465 --> 00:29:04.447
And that’s it.

518
00:29:04.447 --> 00:29:07.396
But then . . . You all want to know about Mossville.

519
00:29:07.396 --> 00:29:11.000
You all don’t want to know about all that, huh?

520
00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.653
<v ARSENEAULT>I do want to know about . . . I’m curious about your mom and dad.

521
00:29:13.653 --> 00:29:15.694
Can you tell me about your parents a little bit?

522
00:29:15.694 --> 00:29:16.306
<v GUIDRY>Okay, my parents.

523
00:29:16.306 --> 00:29:17.531
I don’t have . . . My dad died.

524
00:29:17.531 --> 00:29:18.347
My daddy got killed.

525
00:29:18.347 --> 00:29:18.959
My mother had . . .

526
00:29:18.959 --> 00:29:19.776
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were they born?

527
00:29:19.776 --> 00:29:19.980
<v GUIDRY>Pardon?

528
00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were they born?

529
00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:21.706
<v GUIDRY>In [?] Louisiana.

530
00:29:21.706 --> 00:29:27.002
Wait, therefore see my daddy . . . I don't know, I must have been three years old.

531
00:29:27.002 --> 00:29:30.179
My sister was two and my brother was one.

532
00:29:30.179 --> 00:29:33.004
My daddy, he was messing up, I think.

533
00:29:33.004 --> 00:29:35.828
I think his mother . . . his girlfriend killed him.

534
00:29:35.828 --> 00:29:37.593
My mother never did remarry.

535
00:29:37.593 --> 00:29:40.771
Just like I’m never going to get married again.

536
00:29:40.771 --> 00:29:42.889
But my mother was a person.

537
00:29:42.889 --> 00:29:45.007
She was raised by good parents.

538
00:29:45.007 --> 00:29:49.244
The thing is, she took care of us the best she could.

539
00:29:49.244 --> 00:29:51.715
She didn’t let us starve or nothing.

540
00:29:51.715 --> 00:29:53.480
You know what I’m saying?

541
00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:59.482
And then whatever, and I told you, the person that got me like this is Mrs. Stuart.

542
00:29:59.482 --> 00:30:04.778
And still and all, when I retired, I retired as superintendent and everybody was there.

543
00:30:04.778 --> 00:30:05.837
They were there.

544
00:30:05.837 --> 00:30:07.249
And Mrs. Stuart came.

545
00:30:07.249 --> 00:30:11.486
She and her husband drove from Alexandria to come to my retirement.

546
00:30:11.486 --> 00:30:12.898
And I wasn’t ashamed.

547
00:30:12.898 --> 00:30:20.312
But when I went back to the classroom that afternoon, my coworker said, “Mildred, why did you have to say that?

548
00:30:20.312 --> 00:30:27.020
The superintendent looked around when you said I would like to represent . . . ” I don't know what word I used,

549
00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:33.022
because I had it written down what all I was going to say and I forgot now.

550
00:30:33.022 --> 00:30:38.670
But I said, “I would like to tell the ones where I got to be today.”

551
00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:42.554
And I said, “Would you stand up, Mr. and Mrs. Stuart?”

552
00:30:42.554 --> 00:30:45.025
And he said the superintendent looked around.

553
00:30:45.025 --> 00:30:48.203
I said, “Girl why did you have to say?”

554
00:30:48.203 --> 00:30:49.615
I say, “Well, listen.

555
00:30:49.615 --> 00:30:52.439
I’m not ashamed to where I came from.

556
00:30:52.439 --> 00:30:54.204
But look at me now.

557
00:30:54.204 --> 00:30:57.382
I am not ashamed to where I came from.

558
00:30:57.382 --> 00:31:04.090
I came from . . . If I had stayed around Alexandria, I probably would have started getting . . . I never did drink.

559
00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:06.561
My sister was on alcohol and all.

560
00:31:06.561 --> 00:31:14.328
But me, I probably would have stayed around there, wouldn’t have done nothing, or gotten to be a drinking alcoholic . . . just nothing.

561
00:31:14.328 --> 00:31:16.446
But I am proud of them.

562
00:31:16.446 --> 00:31:20.683
<c.p>And when I start working for them, they saw something in me.

563
00:31:20.683 --> 00:31:25.978
Because when I first started working for Ms. Stuart . . . like my television was over there.

564
00:31:25.978 --> 00:31:27.744
Her kitchen was over there.

565
00:31:27.744 --> 00:31:31.980
And I would see her looking . . . She didn’t trust me at first.

566
00:31:31.980 --> 00:31:33.746
I would see her looking.

567
00:31:33.746 --> 00:31:39.041
I think I was ironing that day and I saw her just looking, looking, looking.

568
00:31:39.041 --> 00:31:41.866
I say to myself, “She doesn’t like me.”

569
00:31:41.866 --> 00:31:47.161
But I didn’t say . . . because I always thought all whites how they treated black people.

570
00:31:47.161 --> 00:31:49.633
But all whites are not like that.

571
00:31:49.633 --> 00:31:51.398
They are not like that.

572
00:31:51.398 --> 00:31:52.457
They are not.

573
00:31:52.457 --> 00:31:55.634
You go now, you know, it’s just so different.

574
00:31:55.634 --> 00:32:01.283
And I don’t understand how whites could do . . . When we cut out, we got red blood.

575
00:32:01.283 --> 00:32:07.991
Don’t understand this color of our skin, because if you train them right . . . Just like I’m saying about Mossville.

576
00:32:07.991 --> 00:32:17.170
Mossville people, they are training those children now, if you talk to some of them, you all are going to talk to a lot of them,

577
00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:25.290
you cannot understand, from the community they came from, you can tell that their parents came, most of them came from down east.

578
00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:27.762
But they was thinking about their children.

579
00:32:27.762 --> 00:32:34.116
Most of them saw that school went on and . . . [Right now?] what you all are here for now.

580
00:32:34.116 --> 00:32:38.000
They’re still trying to say not to let Mossville down.

581
00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:47.672
You know, when they closed that way, and the whites had to go and blacks had to go, they still fought for Mossville.

582
00:32:47.672 --> 00:32:54.821
So it just the idea, you don’t just despise people because of the color of their skin.

583
00:32:54.821 --> 00:32:56.923
We do the same thing.

584
00:32:56.923 --> 00:32:57.764
You know?

585
00:32:57.764 --> 00:33:00.287
Well, I don’t have straight hair.

586
00:33:00.287 --> 00:33:02.810
I’m not the color of you.

587
00:33:02.810 --> 00:33:05.333
But God gave us some intelligence.

588
00:33:05.333 --> 00:33:12.062
And then he gave us . . . If you’re not so smart, with your [bones?] being so smart,

589
00:33:12.062 --> 00:33:17.528
well, you’re going to a place where . . . That’s like I said about Mossville,

590
00:33:17.528 --> 00:33:22.154
they’re there, but those parents saw that that school went on,

591
00:33:22.154 --> 00:33:30.144
and saw that their children was going to be not like they came up that didn’t have no education.

592
00:33:30.144 --> 00:33:32.246
You understand what I’m saying?

593
00:33:32.246 --> 00:33:39.395
Because most of those children that had finished from there, most of them got pretty good jobs.

594
00:33:39.395 --> 00:33:42.759
And you don’t find too many of them.

595
00:33:42.759 --> 00:33:44.862
Only one person went there

596
00:33:44.862 --> 00:33:49.067
<c.p>that their child did something to his wife, I think.

597
00:33:49.067 --> 00:33:51.590
I’m not going to name names.

598
00:33:51.590 --> 00:34:00.000
Don’t say . . . because I didn’t want to say that, that he . . . he’s in prison because he killed his wife.

599
00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:03.179
And that’s the only thing I can say about Mossville community,

600
00:34:03.179 --> 00:34:08.092
how they’ve grown and how they’ve grown and how they close together and things and all that.

601
00:34:08.092 --> 00:34:12.428
So I cannot say . . . And that was the best place for me, Mossville High School.

602
00:34:12.428 --> 00:34:13.584
Westwood was the next.

603
00:34:13.584 --> 00:34:17.630
The principal’s there, he start . . . But he couldn't do nothing about [?] just like you.

604
00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:21.387
I can’t do nothing if you’re going to say that nigger Ms. Guidry.

605
00:34:21.387 --> 00:34:22.543
I can’t do anything.

606
00:34:22.543 --> 00:34:23.121
That’s you.

607
00:34:23.121 --> 00:34:24.566
That’s the way you think.

608
00:34:24.566 --> 00:34:26.590
I let God take care of that.

609
00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:27.168
You understand?

610
00:34:27.168 --> 00:34:28.324
Now you little girls,

611
00:34:28.324 --> 00:34:35.260
you all going to have a good talk about when you all get back to you all’s room in the night about Mrs. Guidry.

612
00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:35.838
Ms. Calvin.

613
00:34:35.838 --> 00:34:39.017
Say Ms. Calvin, because they ain’t going to know Ms. Guidry.

614
00:34:39.017 --> 00:34:41.908
They going to say . . . I go by Mildred Calvin Guidry.

615
00:34:41.908 --> 00:34:46.243
They know you’re talking about Mossville, you don’t say no Ms. Guidry, you say Calvin.

616
00:34:46.243 --> 00:34:50.000
Because that’s where my background started from my start of first teaching.

617
00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:51.692
<v ARSENEAULT>What did you teach?

618
00:34:51.692 --> 00:34:54.231
<v GUIDRY>What did I teach over there?

619
00:34:54.231 --> 00:34:55.077
First grade.

620
00:34:55.077 --> 00:34:55.923
First grade.

621
00:34:55.923 --> 00:34:58.038
<v ARSENEAULT>So that’s all the subjects.

622
00:34:58.038 --> 00:34:58.462
<v GUIDRY>Yeah.

623
00:34:58.462 --> 00:34:59.308
First grade.

624
00:34:59.308 --> 00:34:59.731
Yeah.

625
00:34:59.731 --> 00:35:01.423
What did you teach?

626
00:35:01.423 --> 00:35:03.115
What grade you taught?

627
00:35:03.115 --> 00:35:04.385
<v ARSENEAULT>I taught seventh.

628
00:35:04.385 --> 00:35:04.808
<v GUIDRY>Second?

629
00:35:04.808 --> 00:35:05.231
<v ARSENEAULT>Seventh.

630
00:35:05.231 --> 00:35:09.462
<v GUIDRY>Oh, well, see, we’re getting them ready for you all.

631
00:35:09.462 --> 00:35:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You do the hard work.

632
00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:12.626
<v GUIDRY>That’s right.

633
00:35:12.626 --> 00:35:16.693
But see now, when I first start . . . Let me put this in there.

634
00:35:16.693 --> 00:35:20.135
When we first start at Mossville, they did not have kindergarten.

635
00:35:20.135 --> 00:35:21.699
They did not have kindergarten.

636
00:35:21.699 --> 00:35:27.644
So when children first started school, you had to teach them all kindergarten and everything in the first grade.

637
00:35:27.644 --> 00:35:28.270
You understand?

638
00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:29.834
Because they didn’t have kindergarten.

639
00:35:29.834 --> 00:35:33.589
But now they have preschool, they’ve got [?] they get a certain age.

640
00:35:33.589 --> 00:35:36.092
And then from preschool, they go to kindergarten.

641
00:35:36.092 --> 00:35:37.656
From kindergarten to first grade.

642
00:35:37.656 --> 00:35:41.098
They didn’t have all of that back there when I started.

643
00:35:41.098 --> 00:35:43.601
So we had to do all of that.

644
00:35:43.601 --> 00:35:49.546
For who all they had, I’m not bragging, they hired us, and we all did the best we could.

645
00:35:49.546 --> 00:35:51.110
And you saw Ms. Rogers.

646
00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:53.613
That Ms. Rogers, she was excellent in English.

647
00:35:53.613 --> 00:35:55.804
The little lady, the little . . . with the [?].

648
00:35:55.804 --> 00:35:59.558
You know the boy was talking about how he was so bad.

649
00:35:59.558 --> 00:36:03.000
You came to the reception what they invited us to.

650
00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:05.640
<v DIGILORMO>No, ma’am, I wasn’t [on?] yet.

651
00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:06.080
<v GUIDRY>Okay.

652
00:36:06.080 --> 00:36:08.280
She was excellent in English.

653
00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:09.600
They had good . . .

654
00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And just for the record that was Jamie Digilormo.

655
00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:14.376
<v GUIDRY>Pardon?

656
00:36:14.376 --> 00:36:14.753
Okay.

657
00:36:14.753 --> 00:36:15.129
Okay.

658
00:36:15.129 --> 00:36:18.894
And what happened, Ms. Rogers taught English, which was excellent.

659
00:36:18.894 --> 00:36:20.400
They had Mr. Jones.

660
00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:21.530
He taught math.

661
00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:22.659
He was excellent.

662
00:36:22.659 --> 00:36:29.813
They had Mrs. . . . she was the lady . . . When she left, she went to be the president of Southern University.

663
00:36:29.813 --> 00:36:30.942
Just passed away.

664
00:36:30.942 --> 00:36:32.448
What that lady’s name?

665
00:36:32.448 --> 00:36:33.201
Oh, God.

666
00:36:33.201 --> 00:36:34.331
I don't know.

667
00:36:34.331 --> 00:36:36.213
But anyway, she taught math.

668
00:36:36.213 --> 00:36:38.472
And then there was Mr. [Offry?].

669
00:36:38.472 --> 00:36:39.602
He taught math.

670
00:36:39.602 --> 00:36:42.990
All of those people was very good in there.

671
00:36:42.990 --> 00:36:48.261
Down where we were, we had Mrs. Calvin, which was a first grade teacher.

672
00:36:48.261 --> 00:36:53.156
I got to be good, maybe because I was around the other two.

673
00:36:53.156 --> 00:36:56.544
There were three first grade teachers at the time.

674
00:36:56.544 --> 00:36:58.050
Mrs. Smith passed away.

675
00:36:58.050 --> 00:36:59.556
Mrs. Lemelle passed away.

676
00:36:59.556 --> 00:37:00.686
And Mrs. Calvin.

677
00:37:00.686 --> 00:37:02.945
We were . . . Oh, and Mrs. Smith.

678
00:37:02.945 --> 00:37:04.074
They’re all dead.

679
00:37:04.074 --> 00:37:06.333
I’m the only one that’s living.

680
00:37:06.333 --> 00:37:09.345
<v ARSENEAULT>Was the Lemelle related to Butch and Julia?

681
00:37:09.345 --> 00:37:11.604
<v GUIDRY>I don’t . . . No, not down here.

682
00:37:11.604 --> 00:37:16.122
She was from . . . Mrs. Lemelle was from . . . that was her married name.

683
00:37:16.122 --> 00:37:18.005
She was from down east.

684
00:37:18.005 --> 00:37:20.264
No, not the Lemelles over there.

685
00:37:20.264 --> 00:37:21.770
That was a different.

686
00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:23.652
She was from Opelousas [Louisiana].

687
00:37:23.652 --> 00:37:31.935
Because Mrs. Lemelle and Mrs. Smith, they taught me how to do good manuscript writing, because they went to a lab school.

688
00:37:31.935 --> 00:37:34.571
See, they went to Grambling and Southern.

689
00:37:34.571 --> 00:37:40.971
See, Grambling and Southern, they have lab schools, just like . . . Did you go to a lab school?

690
00:37:40.971 --> 00:37:46.619
See, I didn't learn how to . . . They didn’t have . . . Jarvis, it wasn’t a lab school.

691
00:37:46.619 --> 00:37:50.007
So I wasn’t really pretty good on the board.

692
00:37:50.007 --> 00:37:57.161
But they would come and do all of my printing on the board, until I got to work there.

693
00:37:57.161 --> 00:37:59.043
And then that was it.

694
00:37:59.043 --> 00:38:07.703
Then we would get together in the afternoon like now and see what it is and what you need help in it, therefore.

695
00:38:07.703 --> 00:38:08.079
And

696
00:38:08.079 --> 00:38:14.480
<c.p>therefore, all the students there, the students, most of them they were crazy about all the teachers.

697
00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:19.374
Nobody going to think nobody had . . . See, this is what I’m talking about.

698
00:38:19.374 --> 00:38:25.775
Mrs. Washington come calling me and offered she was going to call my mother and tell her,

699
00:38:25.775 --> 00:38:32.175
how would I feel if tell my mother that I was messing with a high school boy.

700
00:38:32.175 --> 00:38:36.693
I said, “No,” I said, “he asking me . . . his daddy wouldn’t come,”

701
00:38:36.693 --> 00:38:43.094
I said, “he asked me,” I said, “Ms. Washington, you live right down the street from me.”

702
00:38:43.094 --> 00:38:51.000
Well I [wouldn’t have to go?], but I was living then in Lake Charles, but I would go across there.

703
00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:53.087
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever live in Mossville?

704
00:38:53.087 --> 00:38:54.826
<v GUIDRY>No . . . Yeah, for a while.

705
00:38:54.826 --> 00:38:57.609
I would say maybe a half a year.

706
00:38:57.609 --> 00:39:01.087
Because later I was living with . . . We didn’t get along.

707
00:39:01.087 --> 00:39:04.217
She was something else and we didn’t get along.

708
00:39:04.217 --> 00:39:07.348
So I got me a room in Lake Charles.

709
00:39:07.348 --> 00:39:10.130
I was living a door from the principal.

710
00:39:10.130 --> 00:39:11.870
I was in her neighborhood.

711
00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:16.043
And I never did, no. And I lived over there a while.

712
00:39:16.043 --> 00:39:17.435
But it was difficult.

713
00:39:17.435 --> 00:39:18.478
I don't know.

714
00:39:18.478 --> 00:39:21.957
I don't think I never wanted to live over there.

715
00:39:21.957 --> 00:39:27.522
I liked to go to work there, but I don't think I wanted to live there.

716
00:39:27.522 --> 00:39:28.217
<v ARSENEAULT>Why not?

717
00:39:28.217 --> 00:39:31.000
<v GUIDRY>No, because it was so . . . I don't know!

718
00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:36.217
It was just the idea, I didn’t come from . . . I lived in town in Alexandria.

719
00:39:36.217 --> 00:39:43.174
I didn’t live in . . . see it’s a country town in Alexandria where I’m from, but I didn’t live in there.

720
00:39:43.174 --> 00:39:45.261
I lived in the city part.

721
00:39:45.261 --> 00:39:48.043
And that’s where I was, and why I

722
00:39:48.043 --> 00:39:50.130
<c.p>wanted to live in Lake Charles.

723
00:39:50.130 --> 00:39:55.000
So I would just transfer from over there, from over here, over there.

724
00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:56.117
<v ARSENEAULT>So I’m curious.

725
00:39:56.117 --> 00:39:58.723
What was your first impression of Mossville?

726
00:39:58.723 --> 00:40:06.169
<v GUIDRY>My first impression of Mossville was just like I said, a community that thought they were concerned about their children.

727
00:40:06.169 --> 00:40:14.359
And for Mossville, to be Mossville, like a little rural [as they say there?], the parents, they were concerned about their children.

728
00:40:14.359 --> 00:40:19.199
And I can say to me, to me, they didn’t tell me that.

729
00:40:19.199 --> 00:40:21.805
They were mostly from down, down east.

730
00:40:21.805 --> 00:40:24.411
But they didn’t think about down east.

731
00:40:24.411 --> 00:40:28.134
And down east, to me, would have been good, too.

732
00:40:28.134 --> 00:40:34.091
Because most of the people there, down east, they would go to a predominant Catholic school.

733
00:40:34.091 --> 00:40:37.814
And at that time, it mostly had whites, you know.

734
00:40:37.814 --> 00:40:40.048
The sisters and nuns and things.

735
00:40:40.048 --> 00:40:42.654
And that’s where they went to school.

736
00:40:42.654 --> 00:40:47.121
Maybe that was instilled in them, like it was instilled in me.

737
00:40:47.121 --> 00:40:49.727
I want the best for my child.

738
00:40:49.727 --> 00:40:52.706
Maybe when they went, their parents went there.

739
00:40:52.706 --> 00:40:57.545
And maybe the things weren’t as good as there when, you know, there.

740
00:40:57.545 --> 00:41:01.268
So they didn’t want the same thing for their child.

741
00:41:01.268 --> 00:41:02.758
They wanted something different.

742
00:41:02.758 --> 00:41:07.597
So that’s the way, I think that’s the way it was with Mossville.

743
00:41:07.597 --> 00:41:11.320
Because if you taught . . . I don't know, you wasn’t there.

744
00:41:11.320 --> 00:41:15.043
You would see how they were everything, how they were.

745
00:41:15.043 --> 00:41:21.000
If you visit any of the homes or anything, that’s when you can kind of say,

746
00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:26.212
well this right here, these people here seem to be concerned about their community.

747
00:41:26.212 --> 00:41:29.190
I mean, and they concerned about their children.

748
00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:35.147
They think about later in life, how they going to be able to do as adults.

749
00:41:35.147 --> 00:41:37.009
That’s the way I thought.

750
00:41:37.009 --> 00:41:40.359
I didn’t think about myself too much about after.

751
00:41:40.359 --> 00:41:44.082
I didn’t want my daughter speaking flack like I did.

752
00:41:44.082 --> 00:41:49.294
Now you all can say . . . I don’t care what you all put down there.

753
00:41:49.294 --> 00:41:53.390
But I had children when I taught . . . Please don’t put this.

754
00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:55.623
The difference I could see when

755
00:41:55.623 --> 00:42:01.952
<c.p>I went to Westwood, the children there, they was exposed to a lot of, a lot of,

756
00:42:01.952 --> 00:42:10.143
a lot of different things because their parents, these parents at Mossville, I don't think they all was able to do that.

757
00:42:10.143 --> 00:42:14.238
Because a lot of them, they had nice homes and things.

758
00:42:14.238 --> 00:42:16.844
They probably got jobs at the plants.

759
00:42:16.844 --> 00:42:20.939
Some of them probably didn’t get the jobs at the plants.

760
00:42:20.939 --> 00:42:28.385
But when I went to Westwood, you could tell about parents was concerned, and most of the ladies didn’t work.

761
00:42:28.385 --> 00:42:31.736
But their husbands had good jobs at the plant.

762
00:42:31.736 --> 00:42:36.203
And therefore Mossville, after Mossville got there, too, they start building up.

763
00:42:36.203 --> 00:42:39.554
You know, building up, being concerned about their children.

764
00:42:39.554 --> 00:42:47.000
Having little plays and everything where the children has got, they’d be exposed, and we’d go there and see.

765
00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:53.438
The closeness of Mossville, I think the closeness of Mossville is never . . . it’s not going to be the same,

766
00:42:53.438 --> 00:42:55.810
anyway, because all those plants over there.

767
00:42:55.810 --> 00:42:58.860
I don't think it will ever be like that.

768
00:42:58.860 --> 00:43:04.620
And if you were to come to a lot of little things that they had during that,

769
00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:11.058
you would probably would have seen what I’m trying to explain to you about what I got on Mossville.

770
00:43:11.058 --> 00:43:13.769
And that’s why I stayed there so long.

771
00:43:13.769 --> 00:43:19.868
But you see for like now, I wanted my child to go, I would have put my child,

772
00:43:19.868 --> 00:43:25.628
just like you, if you have, you want to put your child in the best teacher room.

773
00:43:25.628 --> 00:43:27.322
I would have done that.

774
00:43:27.322 --> 00:43:34.777
But my child would have been exposed to a lot of children, at the time, to me, speaking badly like I did.

775
00:43:34.777 --> 00:43:37.826
I didn’t want my child to be like that.

776
00:43:37.826 --> 00:43:38.843
I did not.

777
00:43:38.843 --> 00:43:46.298
So therefore she went to this predominant . . . she has always . . . I sent her to a predominant . . . when she was just in pre-K.

778
00:43:46.298 --> 00:43:52.397
When she got there . . . well, didn’t . . . They was not, because Catholics supposed to be more closely, I guess.

779
00:43:52.397 --> 00:43:56.463
They didn’t do nothing to . . . They didn’t do anything to my child.

780
00:43:56.463 --> 00:43:57.818
It was nothing there.

781
00:43:57.818 --> 00:44:01.545
Because her best little friend was a white, two of them.

782
00:44:01.545 --> 00:44:06.628
Because they came and spent a night with Monica and brought their dog over here.

783
00:44:06.628 --> 00:44:10.694
It was Dr. Churchman’s daughter and that was her best little friend.

784
00:44:10.694 --> 00:44:14.083
I wanted her not to speak all flat and ugly.

785
00:44:14.083 --> 00:44:19.165
And if she was around, not in Mossville, because they don’t expose them, got out.

786
00:44:19.165 --> 00:44:21.537
It was the idea I didn’t want

787
00:44:21.537 --> 00:44:24.926
<c.p>them speaking like the other children around where I was.

788
00:44:24.926 --> 00:44:25.603
You understand?

789
00:44:25.603 --> 00:44:27.298
You understand what I’m saying?

790
00:44:27.298 --> 00:44:32.041
And that’s the reason I work so hard, because it wasn’t no better teachers.

791
00:44:32.041 --> 00:44:34.752
But it was the idea of being around.

792
00:44:34.752 --> 00:44:39.157
If you be around somebody, you’re going to catch on what they’re saying.

793
00:44:39.157 --> 00:44:39.835
You understand?

794
00:44:39.835 --> 00:44:47.967
If I be around you a lot, I would start talking like you, if I was younger, if I wouldn’t be eighty-one years old.

795
00:44:47.967 --> 00:44:50.000
You understand what I’m saying?

796
00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:53.443
And that’s the reason, and that’s what I say about Mossville.

797
00:44:53.443 --> 00:44:56.886
That it was . . . I hope that if they have another community,

798
00:44:56.886 --> 00:45:00.955
they would get along like that community, those parents and everything out there.

799
00:45:00.955 --> 00:45:04.398
I don't know . . . When my back is turned, I don't know.

800
00:45:04.398 --> 00:45:10.971
But you haven’t heard, I don’t hear nothing too bad about what happened at Mossville, you know, since I been away.

801
00:45:10.971 --> 00:45:15.040
And when I lived there . . . I didn’t really live there all my years,

802
00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:20.048
I only lived there like a year, and then I moved across, back in Lake Charles.

803
00:45:20.048 --> 00:45:23.491
And I was living, that’s how that boy was asking me,

804
00:45:23.491 --> 00:45:29.751
because he lived over here but they wanted him to stay at Mossville because he was a good basketball player.

805
00:45:29.751 --> 00:45:31.942
You see, that’s the way it was.

806
00:45:31.942 --> 00:45:35.698
He didn’t have a ride, and he asked me could he ride.

807
00:45:35.698 --> 00:45:41.958
And I think of who it was that started that, it was an older guy was trying to like me.

808
00:45:41.958 --> 00:45:44.775
And I think he told that to Ms. Washington.

809
00:45:44.775 --> 00:45:47.905
But she never did tell me who told her that.

810
00:45:47.905 --> 00:45:48.531
She wouldn’t.

811
00:45:48.531 --> 00:45:50.721
And she used to ride with me.

812
00:45:50.721 --> 00:45:52.912
But she never did tell me that.

813
00:45:52.912 --> 00:45:58.546
She just told me that morning, “Ms. Calvin, I know you work hard and you’re a good teacher,

814
00:45:58.546 --> 00:46:04.180
but I would have to write and tell your mother that you’re messing with a high school boy.”

815
00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:07.623
I said, “Ms. Washington, do you know me better than that?”

816
00:46:07.623 --> 00:46:09.814
I said, “No, who you talking about?”

817
00:46:09.814 --> 00:46:13.570
“You know who I’m talking,” I said, “You’re talking about George Kelly?

818
00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:17.013
I said George Kelly live right down the street from you.”

819
00:46:17.013 --> 00:46:20.456
I said, “Maybe . . .” I say, “Why would I want George Kelly?”

820
00:46:20.456 --> 00:46:22.960
She said, “Well, that’s what they are saying.”

821
00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:28.281
So it didn’t [went to congress?] because you know, she never did have to call my mother.

822
00:46:28.281 --> 00:46:31.724
And it never was put on out to the school board.

823
00:46:31.724 --> 00:46:32.350
Because, I

824
00:46:32.350 --> 00:46:36.732
<c.p>don't know how they would do nothing, because you know, I’m reading . . . this lady,

825
00:46:36.732 --> 00:46:41.114
they say she was the best teacher in the world at the charter school,

826
00:46:41.114 --> 00:46:47.374
and she was messing with a, and they would say she’s an excellent teacher, but she like those little boys.

827
00:46:47.374 --> 00:46:48.000
No.

828
00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:50.676
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have a favorite memory of Mossville?

829
00:46:50.676 --> 00:46:51.345
<v GUIDRY>Favorite memory.

830
00:46:51.345 --> 00:46:58.034
I used to like when Mossville what they’d do during the time, senior time, when the children in high school.

831
00:46:58.034 --> 00:47:05.393
I like the idea when they were fixed us, all of us had to wear caps and gowns and everything like that.

832
00:47:05.393 --> 00:47:08.403
Just the caps and gowns and all that thing.

833
00:47:08.403 --> 00:47:12.417
You know, where you all . . . that have the tassels and all that.

834
00:47:12.417 --> 00:47:14.090
I used to like that.

835
00:47:14.090 --> 00:47:18.772
And all the way the children and the parents respect you as a teacher.

836
00:47:18.772 --> 00:47:26.131
They didn’t . . . They didn’t never respect you as nothing, you know how some of them they don’t have respect for teaches now.

837
00:47:26.131 --> 00:47:27.469
They didn’t do that.

838
00:47:27.469 --> 00:47:30.479
I had the experience of that in that community.

839
00:47:30.479 --> 00:47:36.166
And I don't know if it’s another community that I didn’t experience, there’s a community like Mossville.

840
00:47:36.166 --> 00:47:40.179
Because when I left there, you see, I went to Cherry Street.

841
00:47:40.179 --> 00:47:41.517
That’s in town here.

842
00:47:41.517 --> 00:47:45.197
Here, where I went to Cherry Street, that’s a totally different.

843
00:47:45.197 --> 00:47:46.200
That’s totally different.

844
00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:51.552
I wouldn’t say . . . I don’t see too much good there because it was in a town.

845
00:47:51.552 --> 00:47:53.559
And therefore it’s nothing like Mossville.

846
00:47:53.559 --> 00:47:54.897
<v ARSENEAULT>How was it different?

847
00:47:54.897 --> 00:47:57.907
<v GUIDRY>Well, it’s just the idea how the parents were.

848
00:47:57.907 --> 00:48:01.586
They weren’t concerned about how Mossville, they were about those children.

849
00:48:01.586 --> 00:48:05.934
I don't know if it was . . . Don’t put that, but I don't know.

850
00:48:05.934 --> 00:48:11.621
I don't know if it was there because the parents were all from down east, and they

851
00:48:11.621 --> 00:48:14.631
<c.p>probably didn’t go to school or nothing like that.

852
00:48:14.631 --> 00:48:16.972
And they were very concerned about that.

853
00:48:16.972 --> 00:48:22.324
And also, not only, the school and all, they were concerned about their religion and all.

854
00:48:22.324 --> 00:48:25.000
I mean, their religion and the church.

855
00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:26.522
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean in Mossville?

856
00:48:26.522 --> 00:48:27.663
<v GUIDRY>[Agrees] In Mossville.

857
00:48:27.663 --> 00:48:34.890
I mean, when one of them dies or something, the whole, looked like a . . . the whole church went up.

858
00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:38.314
You know, like the whole, the church be full.

859
00:48:38.314 --> 00:48:42.498
And all of . . . they just respect that, all of them wanted.

860
00:48:42.498 --> 00:48:45.161
The pastor died not too long ago.

861
00:48:45.161 --> 00:48:49.345
And I mean, they couldn’t have his funeral at the church.

862
00:48:49.345 --> 00:48:52.769
They had to have it at a bigger church.

863
00:48:52.769 --> 00:48:54.290
<v ARSENEAULT>What pastor was that?

864
00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:56.573
<v GUIDRY>Let’s me see, what’s his name?

865
00:48:56.573 --> 00:49:02.659
They named him, when we were meeting he had, I don't know, I don't remember. [Mr.

866
00:49:02.659 --> 00:49:04.180
Achen?] You remember him?

867
00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:08.365
Mr. [Achen?] died not too long ago I think it was.

868
00:49:08.365 --> 00:49:14.831
It’s about two years ago he had . . . And they had . . . That’s the kind of respect they had.

869
00:49:14.831 --> 00:49:20.157
I mean, they had to have it in the biggest church in Lake Charles,

870
00:49:20.157 --> 00:49:27.384
because there were so many people for the community over there and they knew that how nice he was.

871
00:49:27.384 --> 00:49:32.710
A lot of people from Lake Charles also wanted to go to his funeral.

872
00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:36.514
But I just loved it at Mossville, I tell you.

873
00:49:36.514 --> 00:49:38.796
But that was my first experience.

874
00:49:38.796 --> 00:49:41.839
That’s what the superintendent hired me to do,

875
00:49:41.839 --> 00:49:48.306
to give me a job to start better in my life in . . . What word would I use?

876
00:49:48.306 --> 00:49:50.969
In material things and things like that.

877
00:49:50.969 --> 00:49:54.773
And that’s where I got my first start from there.

878
00:49:54.773 --> 00:49:59.337
And I stayed there until it was time for me to retire.

879
00:49:59.337 --> 00:50:02.000
But I would have stayed there . . .

880
00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:06.094
<c.p>Then they started liking me to parents, really had started liking me at Westwood.

881
00:50:06.094 --> 00:50:11.066
But it’s the idea that when that little girl say that, I don't know how it was.

882
00:50:11.066 --> 00:50:13.406
Because I had the parents all the time.

883
00:50:13.406 --> 00:50:16.623
You not in . . . or you up there with the bigger children.

884
00:50:16.623 --> 00:50:18.670
They had to get a room mother.

885
00:50:18.670 --> 00:50:22.179
They was always, I didn’t have to fool with none of that.

886
00:50:22.179 --> 00:50:26.274
And then like one of them made my daughter’s skirt to go to school,

887
00:50:26.274 --> 00:50:30.368
you know go to Saint Margaret’s, they had to wear them uniforms and things.

888
00:50:30.368 --> 00:50:31.538
They were very expensive.

889
00:50:31.538 --> 00:50:36.509
And I saw that she could do, and I asked her will she do, and she did.

890
00:50:36.509 --> 00:50:37.972
No one charged me anything.

891
00:50:37.972 --> 00:50:39.142
She was real nice.

892
00:50:39.142 --> 00:50:40.311
That was Lisa’s mother.

893
00:50:40.311 --> 00:50:42.943
I don't know what happened to Lisa and them.

894
00:50:42.943 --> 00:50:44.406
But they were very nice.

895
00:50:44.406 --> 00:50:47.330
But they lived in Westlake, but that’s where they were.

896
00:50:47.330 --> 00:50:48.500
They lived in Westlake.

897
00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:51.717
That wasn’t at Mossville now that was at Westlake, the whites.

898
00:50:51.717 --> 00:50:58.151
See, Westlake was something else because I think they’re still having problem because they have like Saint John Catholic Church over there.

899
00:50:58.151 --> 00:51:01.953
And I be talking to one lady and say . . . I said . . . she said,

900
00:51:01.953 --> 00:51:04.000
“No.” I go . . . You all Catholic?

901
00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:05.616
<v DIGILORMO>Half of my family is.

902
00:51:05.616 --> 00:51:11.111
<v GUIDRY>We would come . . . Said that white lady didn’t . . . I’m not going to tell you what she said.

903
00:51:11.111 --> 00:51:11.758
<v DIGILORMO>No, no.

904
00:51:11.758 --> 00:51:13.374
<v GUIDRY>[laughs] They shaking their hand.

905
00:51:13.374 --> 00:51:15.960
We shake our hand and things like that.

906
00:51:15.960 --> 00:51:22.424
I said, “Well, I went to one and one told me . . .” My church over here, you know, with the Catholics,

907
00:51:22.424 --> 00:51:24.687
<c.p>she did the same thing to me.

908
00:51:24.687 --> 00:51:30.182
I’ll tell you what I say . . . Well, I’m not going to put that on what I said.

909
00:51:30.182 --> 00:51:32.121
I didn’t say it to her.

910
00:51:32.121 --> 00:51:36.000
<v DIGILORMO>We can take it out . . . if you want to tell us.

911
00:51:36.000 --> 00:51:39.057
<v GUIDRY>I say, “Fuck you!” [laughs] Just like that.

912
00:51:39.057 --> 00:51:48.228
But [if I wasn’t over there?] I probably would have turned Baptist over there, going there, but I didn’t stay over there long enough.

913
00:51:48.228 --> 00:51:52.813
I’m talking about to be a member of that church, [Achan’s?] Church.

914
00:51:52.813 --> 00:52:01.984
Because most of them go there, they mostly concern, went to school, they finish school, they went and got a little bit more education.

915
00:52:01.984 --> 00:52:11.154
So most of the thing there is I don't think they may could be at Mossville the way, the building like it is now.

916
00:52:11.154 --> 00:52:13.829
And where these plants and all that.

917
00:52:13.829 --> 00:52:18.797
But I don't think another community, maybe, I don't know, could get along.

918
00:52:18.797 --> 00:52:21.854
And I don't think it was no problem.

919
00:52:21.854 --> 00:52:26.439
They was kind of close to whites, a little bit, I think.

920
00:52:26.439 --> 00:52:28.732
I don't know too much about.

921
00:52:28.732 --> 00:52:32.171
But whites never did go to the black school.

922
00:52:32.171 --> 00:52:36.756
But they had to go . . . They brought this up in the meeting.

923
00:52:36.756 --> 00:52:40.959
They had to go to school in Sulphur [Louisiana] or something.

924
00:52:40.959 --> 00:52:47.455
And they built a new school just not to let . . . When it turned out about the integration,

925
00:52:47.455 --> 00:52:53.187
they built a new school just for Mossville students not to go to that school.

926
00:52:53.187 --> 00:52:57.390
They had already built a new school for you all whites.

927
00:52:57.390 --> 00:53:00.829
And they built another school just for the blacks.

928
00:53:00.829 --> 00:53:04.268
I heard this, I don't know if that’s true.

929
00:53:04.268 --> 00:53:10.000
Just for the blacks wouldn’t have to go to school together with the whites.

930
00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:11.363
But it’s better now.

931
00:53:11.363 --> 00:53:12.727
And you know, people [. . . ?].

932
00:53:12.727 --> 00:53:16.476
I lived to be, you know, I’ll be eighty-two in September.

933
00:53:16.476 --> 00:53:18.180
I hope that things change.

934
00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:19.884
But things really have changed.

935
00:53:19.884 --> 00:53:22.611
I think some white people don’t realize that.

936
00:53:22.611 --> 00:53:23.633
Like [President] Obama.

937
00:53:23.633 --> 00:53:23.974
See?

938
00:53:23.974 --> 00:53:24.997
Bringing up Obama.

939
00:53:24.997 --> 00:53:26.701
Obama, he’s a smart man.

940
00:53:26.701 --> 00:53:30.791
But they just don’t want to consider, respect him, because he’s black!

941
00:53:30.791 --> 00:53:37.608
<c.p>Everything those people . . . Don’t do this . . . And then they going to say now that he’s no good all the time,

942
00:53:37.608 --> 00:53:40.675
that he’s . . . What is it that people don’t realize?

943
00:53:40.675 --> 00:53:44.424
Are we . . . We die, our blood is red just like theirs.

944
00:53:44.424 --> 00:53:50.219
And why do you think . . . And there’s some black people that I don’t want to be around.

945
00:53:50.219 --> 00:53:51.923
But it’s the same way.

946
00:53:51.923 --> 00:53:53.968
Why do you think like that?

947
00:53:53.968 --> 00:53:55.331
I don’t understand it.

948
00:53:55.331 --> 00:53:57.376
Why do you think like that?

949
00:53:57.376 --> 00:54:01.807
And see, that’s the way . . . When I went to Mossville, went to Westwood,

950
00:54:01.807 --> 00:54:06.920
it was so different, because it was a joy to go to school every day.

951
00:54:06.920 --> 00:54:09.987
But when I went to Westwood, oh, Lord Jesus!

952
00:54:09.987 --> 00:54:17.145
And especially when that little girl . . . Because I was getting there, I thought I was going to be loving them like.

953
00:54:17.145 --> 00:54:21.916
Because at Mossville, they had some of their . . . like you all two pretty girls,

954
00:54:21.916 --> 00:54:28.392
I can imagine what you all looked like when . . . They had the prettiest little . . . those Guillorys and those Guidry’s,

955
00:54:28.392 --> 00:54:33.846
and not to be, because some Guidry’s are real dark and some of them real white.

956
00:54:33.846 --> 00:54:36.572
They had some of the prettiest little children.

957
00:54:36.572 --> 00:54:40.662
And I just used to like to hug them in the morning.

958
00:54:40.662 --> 00:54:42.026
And just like that.

959
00:54:42.026 --> 00:54:45.093
And I don't know it, I still do it.

960
00:54:45.093 --> 00:54:52.251
But I say, I’ve got to stop that because people will say this woman here is getting up to being nice,

961
00:54:52.251 --> 00:54:56.000
but she going to try to steal my baby. [laughs]

962
00:54:56.000 --> 00:54:58.091
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, we are at an hour.

963
00:54:58.091 --> 00:55:01.926
And we don’t want to take too much of your time.

964
00:55:01.926 --> 00:55:02.274
<v GUIDRY>Okay.

965
00:55:02.274 --> 00:55:03.320
<v ARSENEAULT>One last question.

966
00:55:03.320 --> 00:55:09.594
Is it okay if we come back and do . . . because I have a lot more questions for you.

967
00:55:09.594 --> 00:55:13.080
Is it okay if we come back and talk again?

968
00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:13.429
<v GUIDRY>Oh!

969
00:55:13.429 --> 00:55:16.566
Don’t come at this time with my stories. [laughs]

970
00:55:16.566 --> 00:55:16.914
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

971
00:55:16.914 --> 00:55:19.006
We’ll make a note of that.

972
00:55:19.006 --> 00:55:19.354
<v GUIDRY>Okay.

973
00:55:19.354 --> 00:55:21.097
<v ARSENEAULT>Maybe earlier in the morning?

974
00:55:21.097 --> 00:55:24.931
<v GUIDRY>Well you see, I go to exercise . . . it’s why I’m dressed.

975
00:55:24.931 --> 00:55:29.463
I’ve been having . . . taking . . . I go to exercise class in the morning from,

976
00:55:29.463 --> 00:55:36.086
let’s see, whenever now I go from eight o’clock until like maybe ten, eleven, twelve, you know, like that.

977
00:55:36.086 --> 00:55:40.966
But now I have to be, since I had that stroke, that light stroke,

978
00:55:40.966 --> 00:55:44.103
well the [. . .?] didn’t . . . he thought it was a children.

979
00:55:44.103 --> 00:55:45.497
It wasn’t a children.

980
00:55:45.497 --> 00:55:50.726
I had to tell a story, he said, “You’re not going back in the classroom.”

981
00:55:50.726 --> 00:55:53.514
He thought because I said . . . I told him . . .

982
00:55:53.514 --> 00:55:54.909
<v ARSENEAULT>When you were substituting?

983
00:55:54.909 --> 00:55:55.257
<v GUIDRY>Pardon?

984
00:55:55.257 --> 00:55:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When you were substituting?

985
00:55:57.000 --> 00:55:57.296
<v GUIDRY>Yeah.

986
00:55:57.296 --> 00:56:03.206
But see, he didn’t want me going back subbing anymore because . . . but he wanted me to be around people talking.

987
00:56:03.206 --> 00:56:04.093
See, like this?

988
00:56:04.093 --> 00:56:05.866
This is something for me today,

989
00:56:05.866 --> 00:56:06.752
<c.p>talking to you.

990
00:56:06.752 --> 00:56:10.299
He wanted me to be in a group . . . go to a group.

991
00:56:10.299 --> 00:56:12.663
I told him . . . He said, “They got groups?

992
00:56:12.663 --> 00:56:13.845
You go to groups?”

993
00:56:13.845 --> 00:56:16.504
I said, “No. These groups around here,” I said,

994
00:56:16.504 --> 00:56:22.415
“these whites do not want us in there with them” So this group they found, this is my community center.

995
00:56:22.415 --> 00:56:26.257
So therefore, when we get through with our exercise, we go do beanbags.

996
00:56:26.257 --> 00:56:27.734
You know what that is?

997
00:56:27.734 --> 00:56:28.916
You all played that?

998
00:56:28.916 --> 00:56:35.122
It’s just beans in a little pot, about that size, and they have just like a basketball and you get there,

999
00:56:35.122 --> 00:56:39.260
and you working, you know, you have to do that and you have points.

1000
00:56:39.260 --> 00:56:46.057
And you play against, and it’s a lot of fun because when we win and we lose, it’s a lot of things there.

1001
00:56:46.057 --> 00:56:47.830
Because we playing against each other.

1002
00:56:47.830 --> 00:56:49.603
So that’s where I’m going today.

1003
00:56:49.603 --> 00:56:53.445
But I told him, I said, “The children wasn’t the cause of this.”

1004
00:56:53.445 --> 00:56:55.809
I said, “The children had never gotten there.

1005
00:56:55.809 --> 00:56:58.469
But I was in a pre-k room that day.

1006
00:56:58.469 --> 00:57:03.197
And I was going to the desk to see what that teacher wanted me to do.”

1007
00:57:03.197 --> 00:57:07.630
I said, “Because therefore, teaching pre-k, you have to do everything for the little children.

1008
00:57:07.630 --> 00:57:09.699
They don’t eat breakfast in the morning.

1009
00:57:09.699 --> 00:57:14.722
You have to try to see that they got their breakfast, see where they’re sitting and everything.

1010
00:57:14.722 --> 00:57:16.496
And call the name and everything.”

1011
00:57:16.496 --> 00:57:23.293
So I said . . . he said, “Ms. Guidry,” he said, “I know what you’re saying, but I’m telling you, I am not a teacher.”

1012
00:57:23.293 --> 00:57:26.839
He said, “I’m a doctor,” he said, “but I’ve been around it.”

1013
00:57:26.839 --> 00:57:33.045
He said, “Those little children going to get to you and then you’re going to be just where you are now.”

1014
00:57:33.045 --> 00:57:36.000
Still not to go back in that classroom. [laughs]

1015
00:57:36.000 --> 00:57:36.741
<v DIGILORMO>Well, sometimes . . .

1016
00:57:36.741 --> 00:57:38.593
<v GUIDRY>He said, “Get around people.”

1017
00:57:38.593 --> 00:57:44.148
<v ARSENEAULT>So it’s okay for you . . . the doctor will think it’s okay to talk to us?

1018
00:57:44.148 --> 00:57:44.889
<v GUIDRY>Oh, when?

1019
00:57:44.889 --> 00:57:46.000
No, baby . . .

1020
00:57:46.000 --> 00:57:46.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4490.

1021
00:57:46.429 --> 00:57:46.857
End Session I.]
